.TH PNP-COURIER-SETUP 8 "2026-11-28" "pnp @VERSION@" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
pnp-courier-setup \- prepare an external drive as a pnp courier, self drive or kill card
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sudo pnp-courier-setup
.I DEVICE
.SH DESCRIPTION
Erases the whole external drive
.I DEVICE
(for example /dev/sdb, not a partition) and makes one GPT partition of pnp's own type, with no filesystem.
Nothing mounts it, and no kernel parses a filesystem someone else's laptop wrote; pnp reads and writes it
directly. The udev rule installed with pnp lets members of the
.B pnp
group use such partitions without root.
.PP
External spinning hard disks are preferred for couriers: they overwrite in place, so pnp's overwrite between
visits removes the previous member's data (apart from sectors the disk has remapped). Flash drives (USB sticks,
SSDs) work too, but wear levelling keeps old copies the overwrite can't reach; the script says when a drive is
flash.
.PP
It asks you to type ERASE first, refuses anything that isn't a removable or USB drive, and refuses a drive with
anything mounted. Afterwards, as yourself, run
.B pnp \-\-courier\-check
on the new partition: it writes the whole drive and reads it back, which catches drives that hold less than they
claim (this takes hours on a large disk).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pnp (1),
.BR sgdisk (8)
